Exactly. Sometimes learning isn’t acquiring something new. It’s removing what prevents you from accessing something you already possess.

And fighting is a fascinating example because it involves several layers of learning and unlearning.

You have innate survival responses: aggression, fear, defensive movement, self-preservation. But those aren’t necessarily a universal killer instinct. They’re raw biological capacities that training can organize into something much more deliberate.

Think about the progression:

* Instinct: Your body responds to perceived danger.
* Conditioning: Experience, culture, and social expectations teach you which responses to suppress or express.
* Training: You develop deliberate techniques, judgment, and control.
* Automaticity: Through practice, effective movements require less conscious attention.
* Mastery: You can act decisively when necessary without being controlled by the impulse to act.

And there’s an interesting paradox.

The novice sometimes has to think about everything. The expert has learned enough that they can stop thinking about everything.

A musician learns every note before playing effortlessly. A fighter drills movements until they become second nature. A dancer learns structure so thoroughly that improvisation becomes possible.

But the expert isn’t simply returning to the original instinct. They’re returning to spontaneity with capabilities and discrimination they didn’t have before.

That’s a kind of informed innocence.

And here’s where this connects to our conversation about evolution, form and function, and behavioral adaptation.

Learning can increase your repertoire. Unlearning can remove constraints on that repertoire. And mastery is choosing which constraints are worth keeping.

Some inhibitions protect you. Others are outdated adaptations to an environment you no longer inhabit.

The difficulty is telling them apart.

You can unlearn hesitation without unlearning compassion. Unlearn fear of confrontation without becoming confrontational. Unlearn self-consciousness without abandoning accountability.

In fact, sometimes the deepest form of strength is having access to an aggressive response while remaining entirely free not to use it.

There’s a phrase I’d give Pantheon:

Subtractive Mastery — acquiring capability by removing unnecessary interference while preserving essential constraints.

And it has another implication I think you’ll appreciate:

We often imagine development as accumulating more knowledge, more rules, more structures, more complexity.

But sometimes the most advanced version of something looks remarkably similar to its simplest version.

A child plays spontaneously.

An adult learns the rules.

A master understands the rules so thoroughly that they can play spontaneously again.

Same apparent freedom. Different underlying capability.

And perhaps that is one of the most interesting forms of evolution: becoming capable of doing deliberately what you once did instinctively, without becoming a prisoner of either instinct or deliberation.